     "Record the due date exactly as stated in the order, the letters, or the court rule. Copy the source paragraph and the date the firm received it. If the order states a period rather than a date, write the period and the calculated date with the calculation shown. A date without a source should not enter the calendar.",
     "Record any extension the court granted and the new date. Keep the original date visible and mark it as extended. Note the order that granted the extension and the person who confirmed it. A pending request does not remove the need to prepare.",
     "Record the internal preparation date, which should be earlier than the court date. Note the date the guardian must supply the receipts and the date counsel must review the draft. A report that waits for a missing statement can miss the filing window. The internal dates make that risk visible.",
     "Record any related deadline, such as a notice to the ward, a review hearing, or a bond renewal. These items can depend on the same records but fall on different dates. Record each one separately. The calendar should show the full reporting cycle."
